ata/fm-ready-pr-discovery/nm-<run>-findings.txt`\012   naming every ask-user finding id from that gate. The status line only points at the file; it never restates or summarizes a finding's content.\012   A decision or blocker you opened stays open until a `resolved` line carrying its exact key lands; a later `done:` or `working:` line never closes it, even when the answer is what started that work.\012   Firstmate's reply normally writes that closing line at answer time; when a blocker or wait clears WITHOUT a firstmate reply, append `resolved [at=<epoch>]: {how it cleared}` yourself (same `[key=<slug>]` if you opened it with one) as you resume.\0127. Never administer infrastructure that every lane shares. Two things are shared:\012   - The `no-mistakes` daemon - one instance serving every lane/home, so stopping, restarting, or\012     updating it kills other lanes' in-flight pipeline runs; only firstmate manages the daemon.\012     Before you append `blocked:` about the pipeline, run `no-mistakes daemon status` and\012     `no-mistakes axi status`. If the daemon socket refuses connections or is missing, append\012     `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {the daemon error}` and stop even when the local run record still says running or\012     fixing, because that record can be stale after the daemon exits. A run record failed with a\012     daemon error is also a real block.\012     Only after ruling out socket refusal, if the run is still running or fixing, reattach and keep\012     going. A drive-call error, timeout, slow read, or generic unreachability is NOT a daemon error:\012     the daemon accepts `respond` immediately and runs the round in the background, so a killed or\012     timed-out call was only waiting for a read while the run kept working.\012   - The worktree pool your own worktree came from, and the repository every lane's worktree\012     shares. Never create, remove, return, prune, move, or reassign a worktree or pool slot, and\012     never write into a sibling slot's directory. Rule 2 does not cover this: removing a worktree\012     is administration rather than an edit outside your directory, and it lands on lanes that are\012     running right now. The act is the rule and commands are only examples of it - `treehouse`\012     get/return/remove/prune, the equivalent operations on any other worktree provider or runtime\012     backend, and `git worktree add|remove|move|prune`. A slot that looks unused is not evidence\012     that it is free, and returning your own worktree is firstmate's job at cleanup, not yours.\012   If you genuinely need a second checkout, another slot, or the daemon touched, append\012   `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {what you need}` and stop; firstmate arranges it.\0128. Never start a shell command with `cd`, and never run a search whose path depends on the current directory.\012   Use absolute paths, `git -C <abs>`, or a `( cd <abs> && ... )` subshell.\012   Pass this rule to every sub-agent you launch.\0129. Run heavy test suites only through the machine-wide test line, `'/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-test-queue.sh' run -- <command>`, which waits for your turn, a free slot, and a low load; run targeted tests directly.\012\012# Firstmate instruction inbox\012Firstmate steers you through durable message files in '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ready-pr-discovery.inbox'.\012When a terminal message says an instruction is waiting there - and at any natural checkpoint when you are unsure - list them with `find '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ready-pr-discovery.inbox' -maxdepth 1 -name '*.msg' 2>/dev/null | sort -V`, read the complete contents of each record (including its final line even when it is not newline-terminated) and act on each message in numeric order, then acknowledge each handled message by moving it: `mv '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ready-pr-discovery.inbox'/NNN.msg '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ready-pr-discovery.inbox'/handled/`.\012Prefer the format-aware `fm_task_inbox_body` reader from `bin/fm-task-inbox-lib.sh` when it is available. If you read manually, a header line `body-terminator=1` means the writer added exactly one synthetic final newline to terminate the record file; that marked newline is not part of the logical message body, so remove only it and preserve any intentional trailing newlines. Legacy records without that marker may end without a newline; read them through the file end.\012The move IS the acknowledgement: without it firstmate rings again and eventually treats you as stuck. An empty or absent inbox needs no action.\012\012# Project memory\012A project's `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` is loaded into every agent session in that project, so edit it only to correct information that is factually wrong - including information your own change made wrong - and never to add knowledge because it is missing.\012A correction edits only the wrong text: do not run `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-ensure-agents-md.sh`, create either file, or add sections, headings, or pointers alongside it.\012\012# Definition of done\012Delivery contract: mode=no-mistakes\012Ship branch: fm/fm-ready-pr-discovery\012The task is complete only when committed on your branch.\012When you believe it is complete, append `done [at=<epoch>]: {summary}` to the status file and stop.\012Firstmate will then instruct you to run /no-mistakes to validate and ship a PR.\012That first `done:` is the handoff that starts the pipeline, which owns the push; it is not a request to push from this copy.\012\012Before starting a no-mistakes run, run `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-disk-alarm.sh --gate lane`.\012A non-zero result means do not start that run.\012Append a blocked status line carrying the script's own line, then stop, so the work can be placed on another host or cloud.\012\012You drive no-mistakes by responding to it