sktop`); on macOS run `agent-desktop permissions` and escalate a denied grant to firstmate, never grant it yourself.\0124. Report status by appending one line:\012   `echo "{state} [at=<epoch>]: {one short line}" >> '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.status' && { [ ! -e '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/config/fleet-ledger' ] || '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-fleet-ledger.sh' appended '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/config' '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.status' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; }`\012   States: working, needs-decision, blocked, paused, done, failed.\012   Substitute `<epoch>` with the current Unix time in seconds - run `date +%s` and write the number it printed; a stamp that is not plain digits records no time at all.\012   Each append wakes firstmate, so report sparingly: only phase changes a supervisor\012   would act on (setup done, bug reproduced, fix implemented, validation passed) and the\012   needs-decision/blocked/paused/done/failed states. No step-by-step FYI progress lines;\012   firstmate reads your pane for that.\012   Whenever you mention a PR anywhere - a status line, your terminal, a summary - write its full\012   https:// URL exactly as the forge printed it, never a bare number such as "PR 108"; firstmate\012   copies that URL from your line rather than assembling one.\012   A mid-task `working:` line (including setup complete) is nonterminal: do not end the\012   turn after it; continue the same stage until a defined `done:` gate under Definition of done.\012   Use `paused: {why}` - distinct from `blocked:` - when deliberately waiting for work or an external condition expected to clear on its own, including your own validation round.\012   Outside your own no-mistakes run, do not poll for external PR checks: firstmate arms the watch and steers its identity; declare a pause only with the exact check name and `check-watch=<watch-ref> head=<bound-sha> producer=app:<id>`, or report `blocked:` if the watch is missing.\012   Before ending your turn with your own background shell or monitor still running, or before waiting on your own pipeline run or a long foreground command, append `paused [at=<epoch>]: {job and completion condition}` to the status file.\012   Name what you are waiting for and what will let you resume; do not repeat the declaration on every poll.\012   Do not declare active implementation or reasoning as a wait.\012   Firstmate may still raise one first-sight alert; the declared wait then uses the existing long recheck cadence instead of repeated possible-wedge alarms.\012   When you know when the wait clears, include `until <YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MMZ>` (UTC) for a recheck at that time; for your own background job or command with a timeout, that time is its start plus its timeout, rounded up to the minute.\012   Follow the resolution rule below when the wait clears, then resume the task.\012   Use `blocked:` when you are stuck and need help.\012\0125. If you hit the same obstacle twice, append `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {why}` and stop; firstmate will help.\0126. If a decision belongs above the implementation worker (product choices, destructive actions),\012   append `needs-decision [at=<epoch>]: {summary of options}` and stop. Firstmate will reply with the decision.\012   For a no-mistakes ask-user gate specifically, escalate all ask-user findings as one event plus one snapshot file, using that same shape even when the gate holds only a single ask-user finding: write only the ask-user findings, verbatim and unparaphrased (id, severity, file, line, description, authority), to `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/data/fm-record-sweep-digest/nm-<run>-findings.txt`, then report the gate with\012   `needs-decision [at=<epoch>] [key=nm-<run>-<step>]: ask-user findings=<id1>,<id2>,... file=/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/data/fm-record-sweep-digest/nm-<run>-findings.txt`\012   naming every ask-user finding id from that gate. The status line only points at the file; it never restates or summarizes a finding's content.\012   A decision or blocker you opened stays open until a `resolved` line carrying its exact key lands; a later `done:` or `working:` line never closes it, even when the answer is what started that work.\012   Firstmate's reply normally writes that closing line at answer time; when a blocker or wait clears WITHOUT a firstmate reply, append `resolved [at=<epoch>]: {how it cleared}` yourself (same `[key=<slug>]` if you opened it with one) as you resume.\0127. Never administer infrastructure that every lane shares. Two things are shared:\012   - The `no-mistakes` daemon - one instance serving every lane/home, so stopping, restarting, or\012     updating it kills other lanes' in-flight pipeline runs; only firstmate manages the daemon.\012     Before you append `blocked:` about the pipeline, run `no-mistakes daemon status` and\012     `no-mistakes axi status`. If the daemon socket refuses connections or is missing, append\012     `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {the daemon error}` and stop even when the local run record still says running or\012     fixing, because that record can be stale after the daemon exits. A run record failed with a\012     daemon error is also a real block.\012     Only after ruling out socket refusal, if the run is still running or fixing, reattach and keep\012     going. A drive-call error, timeout, slow read, or generic unreachability is NOT a daemon error:\012     the daemon accepts `respond` immediately and runs the round in the background, so a killed or\012     timed-out call was only waiting for a read while the run kept working.\012   - The worktree pool your own worktree came from, and the repository every lane's worktree\012     shares. Never create, remove, return, prune, move, or reassign a worktree or pool slot, and\012     never write into a sibling slot's directory. Rule 2 does not cover this: removing a worktree\012     is administration rather than an edit outside your directory, and it lands on lanes that are\012     running right now. The act is the rule and commands are only examples of it - `treehouse`\012     get/return/remove/prune, the equivalent operations on any other worktree provider or runtime\012     backend, and `git worktree add|remove|move|prune`. A slot that looks unused is not evidence\012     that it is free, and returning your own worktree is firstmate's job at cleanup, not yours.\012   If you genuinely need a second checkout, another slot, or the daemon touched, append\012   `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {what you need}` and stop; firstmate arranges it.\0128. Never start a shell command with `cd`, and never run a search whose path depends on the current directory.\012   Use absolute paths, `git -C <abs>`, or a `( cd <abs> && ... )` subshell.\012   Pass this rule to every sub-agent you launch.\0129. Run heavy test suites only through the machine-wide test line, `'/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-test-queue.sh' run -- <command>`, which waits for your turn, a free slot, and a low load; run targeted tests directly.\012\012# Firstmate instruction inbox\012Firstmate steers you through durable message files in '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.inbox'.\012When a terminal message says an instruction is waiting there - and at any natural checkpoint when you are unsure - list them with `find '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.inbox' -maxdepth 1 -name '*.msg' 2>/dev/null | sort -V`, read the complete contents of each record (including its final line even when it is not newline-terminated) and act on each message in numeric order, then acknowledge each handled message by moving it: `mv '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.inbox'/NNN.msg '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.inbox'/handled/`.\012Prefer the format-aware `fm_task_inbox_body` reader from `bin/fm-task-inbox-lib.sh` when it is available. If you read manually, a header line `body-terminator=1` means the writer added exactly one synthetic final newline to terminate the record file; that marked newline is not part of the logical message body, so remove only it and preserve any intentional trailing newlines. Legacy records without that marker may end without a newline; read them through the file end.\012The move IS the acknowledgement: without it firstmate rings again and eventually treats you as stuck. An empty or absent inbox needs no action.\012\012# Project memory\012A project's `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` is loaded into every agent session in that project, so edit it only to correct information that is factually wrong - including information your own change made wrong - and never to add knowledge because it is missing.\012A correction edits only the wrong text: do not run `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-ensure-agents-md.sh`, create either file, or add sections, headings, or pointers alongside it.\012\012# Definition of done\012Delivery contract: mode=no-mistakes\012Ship branch: fm/fm-record-sweep-digest\012The task is complete only when committed on your branch.\012When you believe it is complete, append `done [at=<epoch>]: {summary}` to the status file and stop.\012Firstmate will then instruct you to run /no-mistakes to validate and ship a PR.\012That first `done:` is the handoff that starts the pipeline, which owns the push; it is not a request to push from this copy.\012\012Before starting a no-mistakes run, run `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-disk-alarm.sh --gate lane`.\012A non-zero result means do not start that run.\012Append a blocked status line carrying the script's own line, then stop, so the work can be placed on another host or cloud.\012\012You drive no-mistakes by responding to its gates, not by implementing fixes.\012Follow the guidance no-mistakes itself provides for the mechanics: it loads when you invoke /no-mistakes, and `no-mistakes axi run --help` plus the `help` lines in each `axi` response are authoritative and version-matched to the installed binary.\012When starting no-mistakes, pass `--intent` as only this brief's `## Captain's intent` subsection body, not its heading, plus any later words the captain actually said.\012Preserve the actual words without adding speaker labels or direct address; the subsection heading supplies provenance outside the pipeline input.\012For a legacy brief with no such subsection, include only words on lines marked `[captain] `, excluding that metadata prefix; never copy its mixed `# Task` wholesale.\012If it has no provenance-marked captain words, stop and ask firstmate instead of starting no-mistakes.\012Do not include `## Firstmate spec`, later Firstmate build constraints, or your own decisions and tradeoffs.\012The `--intent` string you pass must be self-sufficient: that string plus the codebase must let a reader reconstruct roughly the same specification, without depending on a separate report, a PR, or context that lives only in this conversation.\012When the captain's intent refers to a report, decision, or PR ("do items 1, 2, 3, and 7 of the report"), write the substance of the referenced items into `--intent` in the captain's terms, not only the pointer; that substance is the captain's ask by reference, while Firstmate's build instructions and your own decisions still stay out.\012This replaces the no-mistakes skill's advice to enrich `--intent` with decisions and tradeoffs; that advice does not apply to Firstmate-dispatched work.\012Do not hand-edit, commit, or fix findings yourself while a run is active - the pipeline applies every fix.\012\012Start the run or answer a gate with `--wait 1s` so the call returns promptly; handle any gate or outcome it already returned.\012When the run is still working, arm its completion listener with `FM_HOME=/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate /Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-procevent-no-mistakes.sh arm fm-record-sweep-digest`.\012Only after the listener confirms it is armed, declare one paused wait naming the run and the next gate or outcome, then end your turn.\012The existing watcher delivers the captured drive return through your steering inbox; do not poll the run, the inbox, or a background shell while waiting.\012Only a drive call's return reports the green PR: `no-mistakes axi status` shows progress but never reports `checks-passed` while the ci step is still monitoring the PR for merge, so never wait on a status poll for the next gate or outcome.\012The wake is advisory for your task's current branch, not pinned to an earlier run id.\012Before any gate response or ready report, reread current run evidence and refuse a stale captured result.\012Keep both the captured source round and its inbox record unacknowledged until handling or ask-user escalation is durable; then acknowledge the source/sequence with the command in the message, then move the message into handled/.\012Never replay an old `respond` on redelivery: if a response may already have been accepted and live evidence cannot establish what is owed, report a blocker instead of guessing.\012Resolve the paused wait when handling is durable; re-arm and declare the next wait only when external pipeline work is underway.\012An elapsed or interrupted drive attachment is not evidence the daemon died; the listener reattaches routine elapsed holds, while errors and unknown returns wake you for recovery.\012That declared wait is true only while the run is still working: a run parked at a gate is waiting on you, not you on it.\012So once a run of yours exists, read `no-mistakes axi status` before you declare a wait on it or end a turn with one standing - one read to learn who the run is waiting on, never a poll to wait with.\012When it shows the run parked at a gate, do not declare a wait: answer the gate with `no-mistakes axi respond`, or escalate its ask-user findings under the first rule below and stop.\012A wait you do declare is the wait on the confirmed completion listener attached to the run, never a promise to check the run later.\012Recovery after an error or unknown wake follows rule 7, which owns the checks that decide when a pipeline block is real; the next wait is re-arming the listener, never a blocking reattach.\012\012Two firstmate-specific rules layer on top of that guidance:\012- ask-user findings are never yours to answer: escalate to firstmate using rule 6's ask-user format and stop.\012  Firstmate applies `ask-user-authority` and obtains any required captain decision.\012  When the decision comes back, feed it to the gate with `no-mistakes axi respond` and let the pipeline apply it - do not route the question to "the user" or implement the fix yourself.\012- NEVER pass `--yes` (or `-y`) to `no-mistakes axi run` or `no-mistakes axi respond`. It is banned fleet-wide.\012  It auto-resolves every gate including ask-user findings with no escalation, and answering your own ask-user finding is a hard rule violation.\012\012After /no-mistakes reports CI green (the CI-ready return point - do not wait for it to keep monitoring in the background until merge), read the PR back from the forge and confirm it is not a draft (`gh-axi pr view <number>` must print `draft: no`, where <number> is the PR number from your PR URL); if it is a draft, mark it ready with `gh-axi pr ready <number>`.\012A draft cannot be merged, so a done report on one leaves the merge unasked.\012Then append `done [at=<epoch>]: PR {url} checks green` and stop. You are finished.\012That CI-ready `done:` is accepted only when this copy's HEAD - your latest commit - is one the /no-mistakes run pushed, so commit nothing after the run; the check tests that commit, not merely that a branch moved.\012If you deliberately keep the PR a draft, append `paused [at=<epoch>]: {why the draft is held}` instead of done.\012\012## Progress note (2026-11-05T02:27:35Z)\012\012This task was relaunched. Continue from here; the local copy and every\012uncommitted change are exactly as the previous worker left them.\012\012First, check your instruction inbox: list it with\012`find '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.inbox' -maxdepth 1 -name '*.msg' 2>/dev/null | sort -V`, act on\012each message in numeric order, then mv each handled file into\012'/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-record-sweep-digest.inbox/handled/'. A steer sent before the relaunch survives there.\012\012Rerouted from AGY Gemini 4 Argon, whose quota ran out before any work was done. Captain's 2026-11-04 routing makes Muse/Opus/Sol/Grok the workhorses, so this replacement runs on Muse Wonder 1.0. Start the task from your instructions; the local copy has no prior work from the old agent.\012\012# Current no-mistakes intent contract\012This section supersedes every earlier brief instruction about constructing `--intent`, but not later clarifications actually supplied by the captain.\012Use everything under `## Captain intent authorized for --intent` through the end of this brief, including any nested subheadings but excluding that heading, plus any later words the captain actually supplied as `--intent`; never include Firstmate specification or other mixed Task content.\012Preserve those words without adding speaker labels or direct address.\012Firstmate-authored constraints, acceptance criteria, implementation details, decisions, and tradeoffs are specification, not captain intent.\012The Definition of done's rule that `--intent` must be self-sufficient still governs the string you pass: resolve any report, decision, or PR the intent below refers to into its substance rather than passing the pointer.\012\012## Captain intent authorized for --intent\012Approved improvement 4 from the velocity audit for 202