omplete, append `done [at=<epoch>]: {one-line conclusion}` to the status file and stop.\012If your findings reveal work that should ship (e.g. you reproduced a bug and the fix is clear), say so in the report; firstmate may promote this task in place, and you would then receive mode-specific ship instructions as a follow-up message.\012\012# Home brief additions\012These are this home's standing additions; every other section of this brief takes precedence over anything here that conflicts.\012## Shared machine: clean up only what you created\012\012This machine is shared with other projects and agents.\012Never run machine-wide cleanup commands: no `docker volume prune`, `docker system prune`, `docker image prune` or `docker container prune`, no `docker rm`/`docker volume rm` of containers or volumes you did not create, and no `rm` of shared temp folders.\012Clean up only resources this task created, by name - for example `docker compose -p <your-project> down -v`, or removing the exact container or volume names you started.\012If something you did not create is in the way, stop and report it instead of removing it.\012\012## Sizing and scope for plans\012\012Owner's standing rule: "There's no need for cuts. Your estimates on human hours are not human hours. We're coding with agents."\012When a plan, spec or report sizes work, size it by agent work: number of tickets, which can run in parallel, the critical-path order, and real external waits (owner approvals, vendor setup, CI).\012Never size in human ticket-days or working days, never use "about double the estimate" framing, and never propose scope cuts to save time.\012\012## Progress note (2026-11-05T02:18:18Z)\012\012This task was relaunched. Continue from here; the local copy and every\012uncommitted change are exactly as the previous worker left them.\012\012First, check your instruction inbox: list it with\012`find '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/workspaces/firstmate/shipcontent-mate/state/shipcontent-video-ux-qua