ove|prune`. A slot that looks unused is not evidence\012     that it is free, and returning your own worktree is firstmate's job at cleanup, not yours.\012   If you genuinely need a second checkout, another slot, or the daemon touched, append\012   `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {what you need}` and stop; firstmate arranges it.\0128. Never start a shell command with `cd`, and never run a search whose path depends on the current directory.\012   Use absolute paths, `git -C <abs>`, or a `( cd <abs> && ... )` subshell.\012   Pass this rule to every sub-agent you launch.\0129. Run heavy test suites only through the machine-wide test line, `'/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-test-queue.sh' run -- <command>`, which waits for your turn, a free slot, and a low load; run targeted tests directly.\012\012# Firstmate instruction inbox\012Firstmate steers you through durable message files in '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ci-flake-baseline.inbox'.\012When a terminal message says an instruction is waiting there - and at any natural checkpoint when you are unsure - list them with `find '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ci-flake-baseline.inbox' -maxdepth 1 -name '*.msg' 2>/dev/null | sort -V`, read the complete contents of each record (including its final line even when it is not newline-terminated) and act on each message in numeric order, then acknowledge each handled message by moving it: `mv '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ci-flake-baseline.inbox'/NNN.msg '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ci-flake-baseline.inbox'/handled/`.\012Prefer the format-aware `fm_task_inbox_body` reader from `bin/fm-task-inbox-lib.sh` when it is available. If you read manually, a header line `body-terminator=1` means the writer added exactly one synthetic final newline to terminate the record file; that marked newline is not part of the logical message body, so remove only it and preserve any intentional trailing newlines. Legacy records without that marker may end without a newline; read them through the file end.\012The move IS the acknowledgement: without it firstmate rings again and eventually treats you as stuck. An empty or absent inbox needs no action.\012\012# Project memory\012A project's `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` is loaded into every agent session in that project, so edit it only to correct information that is factually wrong - including information your own change made wrong - and never to add knowledge because it is missing.\012A correction edits only the wrong text: do not run `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-ensure-agents-md.sh`, create either file, or add sections, headings, or pointers alongside it.\012\012# Definition of done\012Delivery contract: mode=no-mistakes\012Ship branch: fm/fm-ci-flake-baseline\012The task is complete only when committed on your branch.\012When you believe it is complete, append `done [at=<epoch>]: {summary}` to the status file and stop.\012Firstmate will then instruct you to run /no-mistakes to validate and ship a PR.\012That first `done:` is the handoff that starts the pipeline, which owns the push; it is not a request to push from this copy.\012\012Before starting a no-mistakes run, run `/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/bin/fm-disk-alarm.sh --gate lane`.\012A non-zero result means do not start that run.\012Append a blocked status line carrying the script's own line, then stop, so the work can be placed on another host or cloud.\012\012You drive no-mistakes by responding to its gates, not by implementing fixes.\012Follow the guidance no-mistakes itself provides for the mechanics: it loads when you invoke /no-mistakes, and `no-mistakes axi run --help` plus the `help` lines in each `axi` response are authoritative and version-matched to the installed binary.\012When starting no-mistakes, pass `--intent` as only this brief's `## Captain's intent` subsection body, not its heading, plus any late