box; do not poll the run, the inbox, or a background shell while waiting.\012Only a drive call's return reports the green PR: `no-mistakes axi status` shows progress but never reports `checks-passed` while the ci step is still monitoring the PR for merge, so never wait on a status poll for the next gate or outcome.\012The wake is advisory for your task's current branch, not pinned to an earlier run id.\012Before any gate response or ready report, reread current run evidence and refuse a stale captured result.\012Keep both the captured source round and its inbox record unacknowledged until handling or ask-user escalation is durable; then acknowledge the source/sequence with the command in the message, then move the message into handled/.\012Never replay an old `respond` on redelivery: if a response may already have been accepted and live evidence cannot establish what is owed, report a blocker instead of guessing.\012Resolve the paused wait when handling is durable; re-arm and declare the next wait only when external pipeline work is underway.\012An elapsed or interrupted drive attachment is not evidence the daemon died; the listener reattaches routine elapsed holds, while errors and unknown returns wake you for recovery.\012That declared wait is true only while the run is still working: a run parked at a gate is waiting on you, not you on it.\012So once a run of yours exists, read `no-mistakes axi status` before you declare a wait on it or end a turn with one standing - one read to learn who the run is waiting on, never a poll to wait with.\012When it shows the run parked at a gate, do not declare a wait: answer the gate with `no-mistakes axi respond`, or escalate its ask-user findings under the first rule below and stop.\012A wait you do declare is the wait on the confirmed completion listener attached to the run, never a promise to check the run later.\012Recovery after an error or unknown wake follows rule 7, which owns the checks that decide when a pipeline block is real; the next wait is re-arming the listener, never a blocking reattach.\012\012Two firstmate-specific rules layer on top of that guidance:\012- ask-user findings are never yours to answer: escalate to firstmate using rule 6's ask-user format and stop.\012  Firstmate applies `ask-user-authority` and obtains any required captain decision.\012  When the decision comes back, feed it to the gate with `no-mistakes axi respond` and let the pipeline apply it - do not route the question to "the user" or implement the fix yourself.\012- NEVER pass `--yes` (or `-y`) to `no-mistakes axi run` or `no-mistakes axi respond`. It is banned fleet-wide.\012  It auto-resolves every gate including ask-user findings with no escalation, and answering your own ask-user finding is a hard rule violation.\012\012After /no-mistakes reports CI green (the CI-ready return point - do not wait for it to keep monitoring in the background until merge), read the PR back from the forge and confirm it is not a draft (`gh-axi pr view <number>` must print `draft: no`, where <number> is the PR number from your PR URL); if it is a draft, mark it ready with `gh-axi pr ready <number>`.\012A draft cannot be merged, so a done report on one leaves the merge unasked.\012Then append `done [at=<epoch>]: PR {url} checks green` and stop. You are finished.\012That CI-ready `done:` is accepted only when this copy's HEAD - your latest commit - is one the /no-mistakes run pushed, so commit nothing after the run; the check tests that commit, not merely that a branch moved.\012If you deliberately keep the PR a draft, append `paused [at=<epoch>]: {why the draft is held}` instead of done.\012\012## Progress note (2026-11-05T02:32:59Z)\012\012This task was relaunched. Continue from here; the local copy and every\012uncommitted change are exactly as the previous worker left them.\012\012First, check your instruction inbox: list it with\012`find '/Users/eduardopicazo/orca/projects/firstmate/state/fm-ci-flake-baseline.inbo